# gh-dash — GitHub PR/issue dashboard

## Launch
    gh dash                          # opens dashboard (your config: defaults to PRs view)
    gh dash --config <file>          # use a specific config file
    gh dash --debug                  # writes debug.log

## Global
    ?         # toggle help
    /         # search (edit current section's query, Enter to run)
    r / R     # refresh section / refresh all sections
    s         # switch PRs view <-> Issues view
    q         # quit

## Navigation
    j / k     # down / up
    h / l     # previous / next section
    g / G     # first / last item

## Selected PR
    a / A     # assign / unassign
    c         # comment
    C         # checkout locally (needs repoPaths set in config)
    d         # view diff (uses pager.diff, default less)
    e         # expand description
    m         # merge
    u         # update branch
    v         # approve (prefills comment from defaults.prApproveComment, "LGTM")
    w         # watch checks (desktop notification on pass/fail)
    W         # mark ready for review
    x / X     # close / reopen

## Selected Issue
    a / A     # assign / unassign
    c         # comment
    C         # checkout branch (needs repoPaths set in config)
    x / X     # close / reopen

## Any selected item
    o         # open on github.com
    y / Y     # copy number / copy URL

## Preview pane
    p / P     # toggle pane / toggle position (right <-> bottom)
    [ / ]     # previous / next tab
    ctrl+d/u  # page down / up

## Your config notes
- Sections: My PRs, Needs My Review, Involved (same pattern for issues)
- repoPaths (work machine only): primeslice/* wildcard, plus an explicit
  override for primeslice/backend -> the stake/pull-requests worktree, since
  the repo folder is a worktree tree (master/bau/current-work/etc.) rather
  than a plain checkout named after the repo. Checkout (C) lands PRs there,
  not on master, so in-progress worktrees stay untouched.
- Home machine: repoPaths is empty — add entries there if/when needed.
